(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-5-pericardial-disease-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Pericardial Disease Quiz 2 — CMS I Exam 5","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 5","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which cancers are named for evaluation when malignancy may lie behind a pericardial effusion?","choices":["Thyroid and kidney cancer","Testicular and cervical cancer","Breast and lung cancer","Prostate and bladder cancer"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. When the cause is unclear, an evaluation for malignancy is considered, for example breast cancer or lung cancer, the cancers named in the effusion workup. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"Which etiology accounts for more than half of cases of constrictive pericarditis?","choices":["Prior chest radiation","Post-cardiac surgery","Connective tissue disease","Idiopathic or viral disease"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. More than 50% of constrictive pericarditis is idiopathic or viral; other causes are post-cardiac surgery, prior radiation, post-infectious disease, connective tissue disease and uremia. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"Besides cardiology and cardiothoracic surgery, which service may an older adult need after a prolonged illness from pericardial disease?","choices":["Nephrology","Infectious disease","Genetic counseling","Rehabilitation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Older adults are more likely to need rehabilitation after prolonged illness, hospitalization and procedures; cardiology and cardiothoracic surgery cover imaging, pericardiocentesis and windows. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"Which pericardial fluid study is rarely helpful in establishing the diagnosis?","choices":["Acid-fast stain and culture","Gram stain and culture","Cytology for malignancy","Cell count and differential"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Cell count and differential are sent but are rarely helpful in establishing the diagnosis; Gram stain, cultures, acid-fast studies and cytology carry more weight. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"In a published African series of acute pericarditis, which cause accounted for most cases?","choices":["Tuberculosis","Idiopathic disease","Neoplastic disease","Autoimmune disease"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Tuberculosis caused about 69.5% of acute pericarditis in the African series, against under 1% in Western Europe, where idiopathic disease (55%) dominated; think tuberculosis where it is prevalent. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Population data suggest a pericardial effusion may be present in up to what share of the general adult population?","choices":["0.5%","6.5%","25%","40%"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Framingham cohort data suggest a pericardial effusion may be present in up to 6.5% of the general adult population, often small and unnoticed by the patient. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"Which imaging test is now recommended for risk stratification of complicated or recurrent pericarditis?","choices":["Ventilation-perfusion scan","Plain chest radiography","Cardiac magnetic resonance","Coronary angiography"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Cardiac magnetic resonance is recommended for risk stratification of complicated or recurrent pericarditis; edema or late gadolinium enhancement shows active inflammation. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"After drainage, what guides further management of a pericardial effusion?","choices":["Treatment of its underlying cause","Lifelong colchicine","Routine pericardiectomy","Indefinite anticoagulation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Further management of a pericardial effusion is whatever medical therapy the underlying etiology requires, such as treatment of cancer, tuberculosis or infection. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"Which laboratory finding is often present in constrictive pericarditis?","choices":["Raised sedimentation rate and C-reactive protein","A natriuretic peptide above a diagnostic cutoff","Positive blood cultures in most patients","Low albumin with heavy proteinuria"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The inflammatory markers erythrocyte sedimentation rate and C-reactive protein are often elevated, while no natriuretic peptide threshold confirms or excludes the diagnosis. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"In the classic electrocardiogram evolution of acute pericarditis, what happens in stage 3?","choices":["The tracing returns fully to normal","Flattened T waves become inverted","Widespread ST elevation with PR depression","ST segments normalize and T waves flatten"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Stage 1 is widespread ST elevation with PR depression, stage 2 normalization of the ST segments with T-wave flattening, stage 3 inversion of the flattened T waves, and stage 4 a return to normal. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"Which chest pain pattern is classic for acute pericarditis?","choices":["Burning, worse after meals and on lying down","Sharp, worse supine, eased by leaning forward","Pressure-like, worse with exertion, eased by rest","Tearing pain that radiates through to the back"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Patients typically have pleuritic (sharp) substernal chest pain that is worse supine and on inspiration and relieved by leaning forward: pericardial pain is pleuritic and positional. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Which is a risk factor for multiple recurrences of pericarditis?","choices":["Mild gadolinium uptake","A prompt viral recovery","An autoimmune etiology","A low C-reactive protein"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Risk factors for multiple recurrences are the magnitude of C-reactive protein elevation, the severity of pericardial late gadolinium enhancement and an autoimmune etiology. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"What adjustment is suggested for colchicine in older adults with pericarditis?","choices":["Avoid it completely","Give it intravenously","Consider dose reduction","Double the usual course"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Older adults call for caution with anti-inflammatory therapy, and colchicine should be considered for dose reduction, as it should for lower body weight. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"Hoarseness with a large pericardial effusion comes from impingement of which structure?","choices":["Left phrenic nerve","Superior vena cava","Thoracic duct","Recurrent laryngeal nerve"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Large effusions are associated with dysphagia, nausea, hiccups, cough and hoarseness, the hoarseness from recurrent laryngeal nerve impingement. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"What is the definitive treatment for cardiac tamponade?","choices":["Expedient pericardiocentesis","High-dose anti-inflammatories","Intravenous fluids alone","Diuresis with a loop diuretic"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Expedient pericardiocentesis is the definitive treatment for cardiac tamponade; it is both therapeutic and an opportunity to send the fluid for diagnostic studies. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"At a second recurrence, low-to-moderate dose prednisone is reserved for which patients?","choices":["Those with post-surgical injury","Those with systemic autoimmune disease","Those with a typical viral prodrome","Those with idiopathic disease"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. At a second recurrence, an interleukin-1 blocker is added for idiopathic or post-cardiac injury disease that is colchicine-resistant with prior C-reactive protein elevation, while prednisone is for underlying systemic autoimmune disease. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"How does a small pericardial effusion typically present?","choices":["Sharp pain eased by leaning forward","No symptoms or a vague, dull chest ache","Sudden hypotension with muffled heart sounds","Hoarseness, hiccups and dysphagia"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A small effusion may be asymptomatic or cause vague symptoms: a dull ache in the chest, fatigue, decreased exercise tolerance or dyspnea. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"Which finding defines treatment failure in acute pericarditis?","choices":["A friction rub that has faded","Mild fatigue after the pain resolved","ST segments that are normalizing","A new pericardial effusion"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Treatment failure is persistence of fever, persistent pleuritic chest pain, a new pericardial effusion or worsening of general illness despite therapy. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"Which treatment addresses uremic pericarditis?","choices":["Corticosteroids","Antituberculous drugs","Pericardiectomy","Dialysis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Treating the underlying disorder is the rule, and for uremic pericarditis that means dialysis, alongside the usual anti-inflammatory approach and close renal follow-up. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"Which feature marks the inflammatory phenotype of recurrent pericarditis?","choices":["Pericardial calcification","A low natriuretic peptide","C-reactive protein above 10 mg/L","A normal C-reactive protein"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The inflammatory phenotype is a high C-reactive protein (above 10 mg/L), fever, and evidence of pericardial inflammation on imaging; these patients benefit from earlier anti-interleukin-1 therapy. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"After pericardiectomy for constrictive pericarditis, roughly what 5-year survival is reported?","choices":["Nearly 20%","Nearly 40%","Nearly 80%","Nearly 99%"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Pericardiectomy gives a 5-year survival of nearly 80% and a 10-year survival of nearly 60%, whereas survival without treatment is low; earlier intervention improves outcomes. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"What does echocardiography usually show in uncomplicated acute pericarditis?","choices":["Thickened pericardium with septal bounce","Right ventricular diastolic collapse","A ballooned apex with a normal base","Normal unless there is an effusion"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The echocardiogram is often normal unless there is an associated pericardial effusion, and the chest radiograph is typically normal; the diagnosis is primarily clinical. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"After a first recurrence of pericarditis, colchicine is typically continued for how long?","choices":["About 2 weeks","About 3 months","6 months or more","Lifelong therapy"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A first recurrence is treated with a nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drug until pain resolves and inflammatory markers normalize, plus colchicine for 6 months or more. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"Which two groups of symptoms characterize constrictive pericarditis?","choices":["Exertional syncope with a murmur","Fluid overload and low cardiac output","Pleuritic pain and a friction rub","Fever with diffuse ST elevation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Symptoms come from fluid overload (peripheral edema, anasarca) and/or diminished cardiac output (fatigue, dyspnea on exertion), because the scarred pericardium limits filling. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"Which echocardiogram finding supports constrictive pericarditis?","choices":["Septal bounce toward the left ventricle","Right ventricular diastolic collapse","Asymmetric septal hypertrophy","Apical ballooning, normal base"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Typical echocardiographic features are increased pericardial thickness, abnormal septal motion or bounce toward the left ventricle, pronounced respiratory variation in filling and moderate biatrial enlargement. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"How often is the complete Beck triad present in cardiac tamponade?","choices":["In only a minority of cases","In nearly every case","In about half of cases","Only in chronic effusions"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The complete classic Beck triad (hypotension, dilated jugular veins, muffled heart sounds) is present in only a minority of cases, so its absence does not exclude tamponade. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"Besides idiopathic disease, which etiology is singled out as especially important behind pericardial effusion and cardiac tamponade?","choices":["Malignancy","Coxsackievirus","Hypertension","Hyperthyroidism"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Neoplastic disease is an important underlying etiology, especially for pericardial effusion and cardiac tamponade, so a large effusion should raise suspicion for cancer. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"Which is the most common disorder involving the pericardium?","choices":["Acute pericarditis","Pericardial effusion","Cardiac tamponade","Constrictive pericarditis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Pericardial disease presents as acute or recurrent pericarditis, effusion without major compromise, or a compressive syndrome, and acute pericarditis is the most common pericardial disorder. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"Most pericardial disease is labeled idiopathic. What are most idiopathic cases thought to be?","choices":["Occult malignancy","Viral infections","Autoimmune disease","Bacterial infections"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Pericardial disease is most often idiopathic, and most idiopathic cases are likely viral; the specific virus is usually never isolated despite a preceding viral illness. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"What is the initial recommended therapy for most patients with acute idiopathic or viral pericarditis?","choices":["Empiric antibiotics plus prednisone","Ibuprofen or aspirin plus colchicine","Prednisone plus a proton pump inhibitor","Anakinra plus colchicine from the outset"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drug (aspirin, ibuprofen or indomethacin) plus colchicine is the initial therapy for most patients, with gastrointestinal protection; prednisone replaces the anti-inflammatory only if it cannot be taken. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 28"}]};
